---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../../../components/Sidebar.astro';
import { frameworkNav } from '../../../lib/sections';
import DataTable from '../../../components/DataTable.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Framework structure"
  description="The definitive anatomy of the UniDPP framework: the fourteen invariants, twelve seams, six layers, service tiers, capability classes, relationship algebra, actor model, registry content classes, and the trust ladder — the named parts of the machine."
  eyebrow="Framework — structure"
  sectioned
>
  <Sidebar slot="rail" heading="Framework" items={frameworkNav} />
  <h1>The UniDPP framework structure</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    One identity and one append-only record per subject, observed through many
    sovereign lenses, served over a degradation ladder, and held together by a
    registered discovery and trust substrate — with issuance, hosting, identity,
    anchoring, archiving and trust as separable duties. It is a structure, not a
    server: every function is a registered, contestable service, and the framework
    specifies the contracts between them.
  </p>

  <section id="anatomy" aria-labelledby="anatomy-h">
    <h2 id="anatomy-h">The named anatomy</h2>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Part', 'Name', 'Content']}
      rows={[
        ['<strong>Doctrine</strong>', 'The 14 invariants (I1–I14)',
         'one-identity-never-reminted · single-definition-many-constraints · finest-recorded-granularity · append-only event sourcing · typed relationship algebra · state-machine legitimacy · issuance-where-duty-attaches · passport-as-projection · graded trust · profiles-as-jurisdiction×sector×characteristic · no-load-bearing-proprietary-layer · enumeration resistance · degradation ladder · continuity'],
        ['<strong>Joints</strong>', 'The 12 seams (S1–S12)',
         'carrier↔resolver · resolver↔hosts · core↔profiles (the mount) · profile↔registry · registry↔registry · log↔transparency-logs · credentials↔trust-lists · transforms↔engines · parent↔child · device↔system · Tier-A↔Tier-B · passport↔adjacent-regimes. Defects live at seams, not components'],
        ['<strong>Planes</strong>', 'L0–L6',
         'identity (15459 + lattice) · neutral core passport · profiles (lenses) · semantic registry (FERIN) · trust (SIGNATIF) · resolution · national/regional serving'],
        ['<strong>Service ladder</strong>', 'Tier A / B / C',
         'carrier-embedded offline minimum · served full passport · notarized archive. Degradation is explicit, never silent'],
        ['<strong>Subject capability</strong>', 'S0–S3',
         'silent · passive-auth · logged-contact · connected. Profiles must be satisfiable by the class'],
        ['<strong>Graph algebra</strong>', 'R1–R7 + E0–E15',
         'association · derivation · installation · type-lineage · profile-of · custody · membership; typed events from issuance through decompose'],
        ['<strong>Actors</strong>', '30 roles, 4 matrices',
         'issuance · lifecycle · verification (incl. marketplace, CAB) · infrastructure; may-issue / may-append / may-read / may-operate'],
        ['<strong>Registry content</strong>', 'C1–C8',
         'semantics · profile shapes · services · protocol bindings · verification mechanisms · transforms/equivalences · predicates · identifier schemes'],
        ['<strong>Trust</strong>', 'SIGNATIF ladder',
         'unsigned → self-declared → attested → multi-signed → log-anchored; threshold roots; M-of-K master list; reason-coded retroactivity; three verification readings'],
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="omitted" aria-labelledby="omitted-h">
    <h2 id="omitted-h">What simplistic DPP models omit</h2>
    <p>
      A common formulation — <em>scan a code, look it up in a discovery service, read
      the passport</em> — works for a first-sale, online, single-owner,
      single-market, intact product. The framework describes the same object across
      its whole life, all its owners, all its markets, and all its failures.
      Each row is an assumption the naive model makes, the reality that breaks it,
      and where the framework carries it.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={['Naive assumption', 'Reality that breaks it', 'Framework carrier']}
      rows={[
        ['First sale, one owner', 'Resale, gift, inheritance, bankruptcy, used-export', 'E1 custody ceremony; N-owner trail'],
        ['Owner identifiable', 'Auctions: judged condition, provenance gaps, tainted title', 'E14 stamps; gap markers; title taint as graph event; gates as predicate runners'],
        ['Product unchanged', 'Repair, firmware, modification — legal class changes with a dongle', 'E4 → derived type + mandatory re-evaluation'],
        ['Product intact', 'Refurbish, remanufacture, decompose, recycle', 'R2 algebra; end-of-waste = new issuance; mass balance'],
        ['Recall reaches buyer', 'Second-hand markets, scattered custodians', 'Predicate recall; DAG traversal; no enumeration'],
        ['Finder is generic public', 'Roles: consumer, repairer, customs, insurer, machine', 'Visibility classes; 30-role model; access as profile content'],
        ['One jurisdiction', 'EU+JP+CN duty on one object simultaneously', 'Three-axial profiles; dated applicability; sovereign suites co-signed'],
        ['Always online', 'Border checks, outages, censored networks', 'Tier-A offline + cached anchors + inclusion proofs'],
        ['Host is eternal', 'Issuer/host death, succession', 'I14 continuity; archive as resolution target'],
        ['One identifier world', 'GS1 / Handle / Ecode / MA coexistence', 'C8 schemes + registered bridges'],
        ['Lookup is enough', '"What was true when sold in 2028?"', 'Event sourcing + snapshots + three readings'],
        ['Silent product = broken', 'Most DPP goods never communicate', 'S0–S3; testimonial-by-default; satisfiability rule'],
        ['Numbers are numbers', 'Boundary classifications flip with decision rules', 'GUM uncertainty; ILAC-G8 guard-banding; registered units'],
        ['Issuers behave', 'Misissuance, hacked authorities, laundering', 'Void-ab-initio; threshold roots; graph taint'],
        ['Finder is human', 'Machines and AI agents', 'Machine-context bindings; acceptance policies'],
        ['DPP stands alone', 'Customs, e-invoicing, EPCIS, UNTP', 'Seam S12; mapping TR'],
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="discovery" aria-labelledby="discovery-h">
    <h2 id="discovery-h">Discovery is a lattice, not a lookup</h2>
    <p>
      The simplistic view — <em>a discovery service maps an identifier to a
      location</em> — assumes the product is on first sale, online, single-hosted,
      single-jurisdiction, intact, and wants to be found. In the framework,
      discovery is distributed across six entry points, routing dimensions, and a
      fallback spine.
    </p>

    <h3>Entry points</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The carrier</strong> — carries an identity that resolves reproducibly, not a URL</li>
      <li><strong>Type-level lookup</strong> — find the model's passport without a specific unit</li>
      <li><strong>Composition traversal</strong> — children discovered through the parent manifest</li>
      <li><strong>Certificate and facility joins</strong> — recall routing, predicate-entitled</li>
      <li><strong>The device itself</strong> — S2/S3 subjects serve their own segment</li>
      <li><strong>Inverted discovery</strong> — predicates: the regulator publishes, custodians evaluate locally</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The fallback spine</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Multi-host linksets</strong> with as-of stamps; cached; inclusion proofs substitute for dead origins</li>
      <li><strong>Succession</strong> — the maker dies in 2035; the registry redirects; the archive becomes the target</li>
      <li><strong>Post-sale relocation</strong> — resale can re-host; the identifier never changes, the linkset does</li>
      <li><strong>As-of discovery</strong> — what was discoverable at time T</li>
      <li><strong>Blind edges</strong> — verify "validly installed in <em>some</em> parent" without learning <em>which</em></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="national-registries" aria-labelledby="natreg-h">
    <h2 id="natreg-h">National registries as peers</h2>
    <p>
      Countries will build DPP registries — the EU's under a forthcoming
      implementing act, China's several platforms already running. The word
      "registry" conflates six functions (identifier allocation, discovery,
      deposit, semantics, trust, surveillance), and the framework already
      separates every one. The national registry is not a rival: it is a
      first-class peer in a federation, and the standard defines the protocol by
      which peers interoperate.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The ePassport precedent, at full strength:</strong> every state
      issues its own passports and inspects everyone else's; ICAO Doc 9303
      supplies the interop and the PKD supplies a passive distribution of public
      keys — a tiny governance table, not a world database. The DPP analogue:
      national registries + the ISO interop layer + the SIGNATIF master list.
      Every delegate carries the artifact that proves the pattern works.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The rule:</strong> global where tiny and governance-shaped (scheme
      namespaces, trust roots, cross-register mappings); national where
      data-shaped (identifiers in use, serving, surveillance). The EU registry,
      in this model, is a peer with jurisdiction=EU — not the root.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
